; Flexible (mobile) electrode, modelled on the project's own reference protocol in
; tests/expE_flexible/pureElectrode_reference/pull.mdp: the electrode is FROZEN
; in-plane and MOVED along z by a pull constraint, rather than integrated freely.
;
; Freezing x/y is what makes this work. Graphite.itp defines the electrode as
; single-atom moleculetypes, so neighbouring carbons carry no exclusions and
; interact through the full LJ potential -- ~1.6e+05 kJ/mol per nearest-neighbour
; pair, ~5e+08 in total. Those forces are in-plane, so freezing x/y removes them;
; releasing all three dimensions (as this case previously did) detonates the sheet
; in one step and the CG then has no converged geometry to solve on.
;
; What this exercises: the electrode geometry CHANGES every step, so the response
; must be rebuilt from current positions -- which is exactly what the matrix-free
; solver is for, and what a cached matrix or frozen splines would get wrong.
userint1  = 2288
userreal1 = 19.79
userreal2 = 4.0
userint2  = 2           ; matrix-free CG -- required when the geometry moves
userint3  = 0
userint4  = 0

freezegrps  = WaL WaU GrO
freezedim   = Y Y Y  Y Y N  Y Y Y   ; WaU released in z only; it is driven by the pull

pull                 = yes
pull-ngroups         = 2
pull-ncoords         = 1
pull-group1-name     = WaL
pull-group2-name     = WaU
pull-coord1-type     = constraint
pull-coord1-geometry = direction
pull-coord1-groups   = 1 2
pull-coord1-dim      = N N Y
pull-coord1-vec      = 0 0 1
pull-coord1-rate     = 5     ; 0.01 nm/step at dt=0.002 -- the geometry changes every
                             ; step (the point of the test) without the 0.5 nm/step
                             ; yank of the reference experiment, which swamps the
                             ; solver comparison with trajectory chaos
pull-coord1-start    = yes
